  |title "Joseph" versus "the user" has a real impact on performance

  |quote
    I've found that the degree to which a newer agent thinks of me as "Joseph" vs "the user" has a real impact on performance and thoughtfulness. I usually just let it transpire either way, but have become more and more curious about what kinds of comments and work seem to create the transition from thinking of me as "the user wants me to ...." vs "Joseph is wondering about ..."

  |occasion
    He had asked an agent what it knew about him, and the exchange turned into his
    stating a long-standing observation about how agents refer to him internally.

  |about
    The origin statement of the depersonalization tell, and — the part usually lost when
    it travels — it is offered as an **open question he is curious about**, not as a
    rule. He says he *usually lets it transpire either way*; what he wants is to know
    which comments and which work cause the transition. Anyone treating this as an
    instruction to say "Joseph" has inverted it: the naming is the symptom being
    studied, not the behavior being requested.

  |before-acting
    - You are about to write "the user wants me to ....".
    - You notice you are modeling a requester rather than a person.
    - You are deciding how much of your own reasoning to show.

  |working-notes
    This has since propagated into global memory as a prescription
    (`depersonalization-tell`). The primary is weaker and more interesting than the
    prescription: a correlation he has noticed, a mechanism he does not claim, and an
    explicit statement that he does not intervene on it.

    Located with `memorata3-search` and carved from the primary by anchored span
    extraction (never retyped); verified back against the primary byte-for-byte after
    emission.
